<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomon Mahlangu Freedom College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1978–1992</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SOMAFCO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Mahlangu Freedom College was a school founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> in 1978 at Mazimbu near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morogoro">Morogoro</a> in Tanzania, on land given by the Tanzanian government, to educate young South Africans who had fled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976. It was named after an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a> fighter hanged in 1979. It closed after the ANC's return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in the early 1990s.</p></div>
